actyly, dysplastic/short nail of halluces, brachytelephalangy, short metacarpals, and toe syndactyly)."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AR","genotype_reason":"biallelic variants"},{"id":"R:37352860:1","pmid":"37352860","label":"Individuals with bi-allelic ERI1 null variants","kind":"group","family":"","n_affected":null,"n_families":null,"sex":"","ancestry":"","consanguinity":"not_stated","onset":"","age_last":"","phenotypes":[{"text":"mild intellectual disability","hpo_label":"Mild intellectual disability","hpo_id":"HP:0001256","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"digital anomalies","hpo_label":"Abnormal digit morphology","hpo_id":"HP:0011297","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia","hpo_label":"Spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia","hpo_id":"HP:0002651","present":false,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false}],"variants":[{"gene":"ERI1","as_reported":"bi-allelic null variants","cdna":"","protein":"","genomic":"","zygosity":"unknown","origin":"unknown","consequence":"null","key":"V5"}],"variant_roles":["causal"],"segregation":"","inheritance":"AR","previously_reported":"","mixed_genotypes":false,"quotes":["A severe spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia (SEMD) was identified in five affected individuals with missense variants but not in those with bi-allelic null variants, who showed mild intellectual disability and digital anomalies."],"quote_grounding":[1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AR","genotype_reason":"stated for the family"},{"id":"R:37352860:2","pmid":"37352860","label":"Individuals with bi-allelic ERI1 missense variants","kind":"group","family":"","n_affected":5,"n_families":null,"sex":"","ancestry":"","consanguinity":"not_stated","onset":"","age_last":"","phenotypes":[{"text":"severe spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia","hpo_label":"Spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia","hpo_id":"HP:0002651","present":true,"n_with":5,"n_assessed":5,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false}],"variants":[{"gene":"ERI1","as_reported":"missense variants","cdna":"","protein":"","genomic":"","zygosity":"unknown","origin":"unknown","consequence":"missense","key":"V6"}],"variant_roles":["uncertain"],"segregation":"","inheritance":"AR","previously_reported":"","mixed_genotypes":false,"quotes":["A severe spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia (SEMD) was identified in five affected individuals with missense variants but not in those with bi-allelic null variants"],"quote_grounding":[1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AR","genotype_reason":"stated for the family"}],"notes":[{"id":"N:37352860:1","pmid":"37352860","section":"description","statement":"Guo et al. (2023) reported eight affected individuals from seven unrelated families with biallelic ERI1 variants. They described a phenotypic dichotomy: individuals with biallelic null variants had mild intellectual disability and digital anomalies, whereas those with missense variants had severe spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["we uncover a phenotypic dichotomy associated with bi-allelic ERI1 variants by reporting eight affected individuals from seven unrelated families.","A severe spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia (SEMD) was identified in five affected individuals with missense variants but not in those with bi-allelic null variants, who showed mild intellectual disability and digital anomalies."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:37352860:2","pmid":"37352860","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Guo et al. (2023) found that the ERI1 missense variants abolished the protein's exoribonuclease activity. This led to defective trimming of the 5.8S rRNA 3-prime end and reduced degradation of replication-dependent histone mRNAs.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["The ERI1 missense variants cause a loss of the exoribonuclease activity, leading to defective trimming of the 5.8S rRNA 3' end and a decreased degradation of replication-dependent histone mRNAs."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:37352860:3","pmid":"37352860","section":"pathogenesis","statement":"Guo et al. (2023) found that iPSCs derived from affected individuals showed impaired in vitro chondrogenesis and downregulation of genes that regulate skeletal patterning. The authors proposed a model in which, within recessive genotypes, missense alleles have a more severe effect than null alleles.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Affected-individual-derived induced pluripotent stem cells (iPSCs) showed impaired in vitro chondrogenesis with downregulation of genes regulating skeletal patterning.","provides a model showing a more severe effect of missense alleles than null alleles within recessive genotypes"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:37352860:4","pmid":"37352860","section":"gene_function","statement":"Guo et al. (2023) noted that ERI1 is a 3-prime-to-5-prime exoribonuclease that acts in RNA metabolic pathways, including 5.8S rRNA processing and the turnover of histone mRNAs.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["ERI1 is a 3'-to-5' exoribonuclease involved in RNA metabolic pathways including 5.8S rRNA processing and turnover of histone mRNAs."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:36208065:1","pmid":"36208065","section":"gene_function","statement":"Hoxha and Aliu (2023) described ERI1 as an evolutionarily conserved 3-prime-to-5-prime exonuclease that works in multiple RNA processing pathways. They noted that no human disease linked to ERI1 variants had been reported before.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["ERI1 is an evolutionary conserved 3'-5' exonuclease with an important function in multiple RNA processing pathways.","the pathology associated with ERI1 variants in humans has remained elusive because no case has been reported so far"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:33942433:1","pmid":"33942433","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Szot et al. (2021) found a homozygous loss-of-function ERI1 variant (c.514C>T, p.Gln172*) in a girl (F5.II.1) who was also homozygous for a KYNU missense variant (p.Glu206Lys) that caused only mild NAD deficiency in a yeast assay. They suggested the ERI1 variant may partly explain her limb reduction defects and cardiac anomalies. However, they noted that the functional link between ERI1 mutation and these defects remained to be assessed.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["Both F5.II.1 and F6.II.4 harbor additional gene variants which may partially explain their distinct phenotypes, despite their suspected relatively mild NAD deficiency caused by KYNU mutation.","Whereas the functional link between ERI1 mutation and these defects remains to be assessed, the impact of these additional variants may confound the contribution of mild NAD deficiency observed in these two patients."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:28488351:1","pmid":"28488351","section":"gene_function","statement":"Choucair et al. (2017) noted that ERI1 is a ribonuclease involved in RNA metabolism that is required for correct skeletal patterning by defining HOXC8 expression, and discussed its possible contribution to the limb abnormalities of their patient.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["ERI1 is a ribonuclease involved in RNA metabolism and is required for the correct patterning of the skeleton by defining the HOXC8 expression."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]}],"dropped":{"records":[],"notes":[]},"gene_decisions":{"candidates":["ERI1"],"confirmed":["ERI1"],"dropped":[],"not_confirmed":[]},"candidate_inheritance":["AR"]}